{"name":"Hibernation","hex":"#6f5166","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hibernation","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of minimum power consumption, inactivity and metabolic depression in some animals during winter.","A hypothetical state of minimum power consumption, inactivity and metabolic depression in humans during long space flights."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hibernation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hibernation","blocks":[{"text":"Hibernation is a state of minimal activity and metabolic reduction entered by some animal species. Hibernation is a seasonal heterothermy characterized by low body-temperature, slow breathing and heart-rate, and low metabolic rate. It is most commonly used to pass through winter months – called overwintering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Eptesicus_nilssonii_hibernating.JPG/330px-Eptesicus_nilssonii_hibernating.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibernation","wikidata":"Q159408"},{"title":"Hibernation (computing)","blocks":[{"text":"Hibernation in computing is powering down a computer while retaining its state. When hibernation begins, the computer saves the contents of its random access memory (RAM) to a hard disk or other non-volatile storage. When the computer is turned on the RAM is restored and the computer is exactly as it was before entering hibernation. Hibernation was first implemented in 1992 and patented by Compaq Computer Corporation in Houston, Texas."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibernation_(computing)","wikidata":"Q681126"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Bat_in_mine_JAPAN.jpg/500px-Bat_in_mine_JAPAN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Bat_in_mine_JAPAN.jpg/1280px-Bat_in_mine_JAPAN.jpg?utm_s